## Why This Question Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to design a real-time monocular system integrating segmentation, 3D pose estimation with scene priors, grasp-point planning, and physics-based stability reasoning—plus efficient edge adaptation with minimal shots.

## Key Concepts

- Monocular 3D pose estimation using environmental priors
- Grasp-point prediction in cluttered scenes
- Differentiable physics modules for stability evaluation
- On-device few-shot adaptation strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode outline for the model heads
function forward(img, shelfPrior) {
  const features = backbone(img);
  const segmentation = segmentationHead(features);
  const pose = poseHead(features, shelfPrior);
  const grasp = graspHead(features, pose);
  const stability = physicsPrior(grasp, pose);
  return { segmentation, pose, grasp, stability };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests few-shot, on-device adaptation, and robust pose estimation under occlusion and motion.

## Key Concepts
- Few-shot on-device adaptation; - monocular 6-DoF pose; - temporal consistency; - synthetic-to-real data.

## Code Example
```javascript
function weightedLoss(posePred, poseGT, maskPred, maskGT){
  const Lp = l2(posePred, poseGT);
  const Lm = crossEntropy(maskPred, maskGT);
  return Lp + Lm;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to more than 3-shot adaptation with minimal latency?
- How would you evaluate failure modes under strong motion blur and fabric reflectivity?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a practical CV pipeline using lightweight, non-ML methods for a common task.

## Key Concepts
- Circle detection with Hough transform
- Radius-based grouping for coin types
- Noise/shadow handling with blur and histogram equalization
- Validation with synthetic data

## Code Example
```javascript
// OpenCV.js-like pseudocode
let src = cv.imread('table.jpg');
cv.cvtColor(src, src, cv.COLOR_RGBA2GRAY);
cv.equalizeHist(src, src);
cv.GaussianBlur(src, new cv.Size(9,9), 2);
let circles = new cv.Mat();
cv.HoughCircles(src, circles, cv.HOUGH_GRADIENT, 1.2, 20, 50, 30, 10, 60);
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you address perspective distortions?
- How would you validate robustness with lighting changes?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design real-time non-rigid 3D reconstruction from monocular input, integrating differentiable rendering and on-device adaptation—critical for AR try-on. It also probes data strategy, evaluation, and edge-latency trade-offs in a commercial retail context.

## Key Concepts
- Monocular non-rigid 3D reconstruction with deformable garments
- Differentiable rendering and photometric consistency
- Temporal fusion for stability
- 5-shot on-device adaptation for unseen fabrics
- Edge latency and data strategy (synthetic+real)

## Code Example
```javascript
function forward(frame, params){
  const latent = encoder(frame, params.enc);
  const mesh = deformableMesh(latent, params.shape);
  const render = renderer(mesh, params.tex);
  const loss = photometric(render, frame) + temporal(mesh);
  return {mesh, loss};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle occlusion or loose fabrics?
- How evaluate mesh quality vs. latency?
- How would you construct synthetic data for textiles?
